{"from": "text:odyssey-19-foot-washing", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-13-04-05-foot-washing", "grade": "C", "note": "Homeric epic shows foot washing of a guest as a household servant's task of hospitality ('She is very feeble now, but she shall wash your feet'): foot washing as a servant's/hospitality task in the ancient Greek world. An earlier, non-Judean parallel - context only (grade C), never the Judean scene or the act itself."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-10-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-13-23-25-beloved-disciple-reclining", "grade": "C", "note": "Pesachim 10:1: 'Even the poorest of Jews should not eat the meal on Passover night until he reclines on his left side, as free and wealthy people recline when they eat' - reclining as the posture of a Judean festive meal. Compiled c. 200 CE, may not describe 30 CE practice exactly, and John itself places this supper before Passover; context, not a report of this scene."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-pesachim-10-3", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-13-26-morsel-dipped-given", "grade": "C", "note": "Pesachim 10:3: the leader 'dips the ḥazeret into water or vinegar' at the seder - food dipped in liquid at a Judean festive meal. Compiled c. 200 CE and Passover-seder specific, while John places this supper before Passover; context only for dipping a morsel at a shared meal."}
{"from": "text:psalm-41-9", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-psalm-41-9"}
{"from": "text:1-samuel-25-41", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-1-samuel-25-41"}
{"from": "text:2-chronicles-24-8-11", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-2-chronicles-24-11"}
